.TH SETVBUF 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
setvbuf, setbuf, setlinebuf \- assign buffering to a stream
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int setvbuf(FILE *" stream ", char *" buf ", int " mode ", size_t " size );
.br
.BI "void setbuf(FILE *" stream ", char *" buf );
.br
.BI "void setlinebuf(FILE *" stream );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The three types of buffering available are unbuffered, line buffered, and
fully buffered.  When an output stream is unbuffered, characters are
written directly to the underlying file descriptor with
.BR write (2)
as soon as they are produced; when line buffered, characters are saved up
in an internal buffer until a newline is written or the buffer is full,
at which point the buffer is flushed; when fully buffered, characters are
saved up until the buffer is full, at which point the buffer is flushed.
.PP
The
.BR setvbuf ()
function may be used to alter the buffering behaviour of
.IR stream .
The
.I mode
argument must be one of the following macros defined in
.RB < stdio.h >:
.TP 8
.B _IONBF
unbuffered.  Each
.BR fwrite (3)
on the stream is translated directly into a single
.BR write (2)
call.  The
.I buf
and
.I size
arguments are ignored.
.TP
.B _IOLBF
line buffered.  Output is accumulated in the stream's buffer and flushed
to the file descriptor when a newline character is written through
.BR fwrite (3)
or when the buffer becomes full.
.TP
.B _IOFBF
fully buffered.  Output is accumulated in the buffer and flushed only
when the buffer becomes full or when the stream is explicitly flushed
with
.BR fflush (3)
or closed with
.BR fclose (3).
This is the default mode for streams returned by
.BR fopen (3)
and
.BR fdopen (3).
.PP
If
.I buf
is non-NULL, it points to a caller-supplied array of
.I size
bytes that the stream will use as its buffer.  The caller is responsible
for ensuring that the storage remains valid for the lifetime of the
stream (or until another
.BR setvbuf ()
call replaces it) and is freed only after the stream has been closed or
its buffer replaced.
.PP
If
.I buf
is
.B NULL
and
.I size
is non-zero (and differs from the stream's current buffer size), the
implementation allocates a new buffer of
.I size
bytes via
.BR malloc (3)
and takes ownership of it; the buffer is freed automatically when the
stream is closed or replaced.
.PP
Any data already buffered for output on
.I stream
is flushed before the new mode and buffer take effect, so
.BR setvbuf ()
should be called immediately after the stream is opened, before any
other I/O operation is performed on it.
.PP
The
.BR setbuf ()
function is exactly equivalent to the call
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
setvbuf(stream, buf, buf ? _IOFBF : _IONBF, BUFSIZ);
.EE
.RE
.PP
That is, passing a non-NULL
.I buf
selects fully buffered mode using the caller-supplied buffer of size
.B BUFSIZ
(1024 bytes on Substrate); passing
.B NULL
disables buffering entirely.
.PP
The
.BR setlinebuf ()
function is exactly equivalent to the call
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
setvbuf(stream, NULL, _IOLBF, 0);
.EE
.RE
.PP
which switches the stream to line-buffered mode while retaining its
existing buffer.
.SS Substrate Implementation
The Substrate implementation of
.BR setvbuf ()
calls
.BR fflush (3)
on
.I stream
before changing modes.  When
.I mode
is
.B _IONBF
the existing buffer is left in place but ignored; the buffer is not
freed until the stream is closed.
.PP
When
.I buf
is non-NULL the previously owned buffer (if any) is released with
.BR free (3)
and the supplied buffer is adopted without copying its contents; when
.I buf
is
.B NULL
and
.I size
matches the existing buffer length, the existing buffer is retained and
only the buffering mode is changed.
.PP
.BR setbuf ()
and
.BR setlinebuf ()
have no return value and therefore cannot report failure; if the
underlying
.BR setvbuf ()
call fails (for example because
.BR malloc (3)
returns
.BR NULL )
the stream's buffering state is left unchanged.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR setvbuf ()
returns 0 on success and a non-zero value on failure.
.PP
.BR setbuf ()
and
.BR setlinebuf ()
do not return a value.
.SH ERRORS
.BR setvbuf ()
fails if:
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I stream
is
.B NULL
or
.I mode
is not one of
.BR _IOFBF ,
.BR _IOLBF ,
or
.BR _IONBF .
The Substrate implementation reports such failures by returning a
non-zero value but does not necessarily set
.I errno .
.TP
.B ENOMEM
.I buf
was
.B NULL
and the implementation could not allocate a replacement buffer of
.I size
bytes.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program associates a fully buffered, caller-supplied 8\ KiB
buffer with
.B stdout
before producing any output, so that bulk writes accumulate in the
buffer until it is full or the stream is flushed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int
main(void)
{
    static char outbuf[8192];

    if (setvbuf(stdout, outbuf, _IOFBF, sizeof(outbuf)) != 0) {
        perror("setvbuf");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++)
        printf("line %d\en", i);

    /* Buffer is flushed automatically when stdout is closed at exit. */
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The next example uses
.BR setlinebuf ()
to make a log stream flush after every newline, which is convenient when
the output is being read interactively or piped to another process:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *log = fopen("/tmp/progress.log", "w");
    if (log == NULL) return 1;

    setlinebuf(log);

    fprintf(log, "stage 1 complete\en");  /* flushed immediately */
    fprintf(log, "stage 2 complete\en");  /* flushed immediately */

    fclose(log);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Finally,
.BR setbuf ()
is used to disable buffering on
.B stderr
so that diagnostics appear without delay:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    setbuf(stderr, NULL);   /* equivalent to setvbuf(stderr, NULL, _IONBF, 0) */
    fprintf(stderr, "starting up\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fclose (3),
.BR fflush (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR fread (3),
.BR fwrite (3),
.BR malloc (3),
.BR stdio (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR setvbuf ()
and
.BR setbuf ()
conform to C89, C99, C11, and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
.BR setlinebuf ()
is a BSD extension also provided by GNU libc; it is not part of the ISO
C or POSIX standards.
.SH COLOPHON
